\title{Universal Libraries, Passage Probabilities, and Optimal Cyclic Indexes}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{15 July 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
A universal finite library is the set of all strings of a fixed length $L$ over an alphabet of size $A$. We separate three questions: how many objects exist, how frequently a prescribed passage occurs, and how efficiently shorter objects can be indexed by overlapping windows. The library contains exactly $A^L$ volumes; prescribing symbols at $d$ distinct positions leaves exactly $A^{L-d}$ compatible volumes; and a fixed pattern of length $m$ occurs at a fixed injective family of positions in exactly $A^{L-m}$ volumes. For contiguous occurrence anywhere, the matching count is at most $(L-m+1)A^{L-m}$, yielding probability at most $(L-m+1)A^{-m}$ under uniform sampling. Exact probabilities depend on self-overlap. We also exhibit the cyclic word $0010203112132233$, whose sixteen length-two cyclic windows list every ordered pair over four symbols exactly once. The construction attains the window-capacity lower bound and illustrates the overlap-graph method. We finally distinguish syntactic occurrence from semantic validity and explain why catalog claims require an explicit coding model.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

A library containing every possible book is finite once its alphabet and common book length are fixed. It is nevertheless so large that existence ceases to be the practical issue. The desired text is present; the difficult tasks are specification, location, and discrimination from near misses. This tension occurs in exhaustive search, information theory, sequence design, signature scanning, and motif detection.

Let $A$ denote alphabet size and $L$ the common volume length. The motivating parameters $A=25$ and $L=1{,}312{,}000$ produce $25^{1{,}312{,}000}$ volumes. We develop the elementary but exact combinatorics behind this model. Global counting leads to local constraint counts; local counts lead to a union bound for passage occurrence; and overlapping windows lead to capacity-optimal cyclic indexes.

A central methodological distinction is between syntax and semantics. Occurrence of a fixed character string is a well-defined finite event. Validity of a proof is not assigned a probability until an encoding, grammar, theorem, acceptance procedure, and resource bound are fixed. The results below concern prescribed symbols and then explain how a semantic language model may be added.

\section{Definitions}

Let $\Sigma$ be a finite alphabet with $|\Sigma|=A$.

\begin{definition}[Volume and universal library]
A volume of length $L$ is a function
\[
s:\{0,1,\ldots,L-1\}\longrightarrow\Sigma.
\]
The universal library $\mathcal L(A,L)$ is the set of all such functions. When $A>0$, the uniform distribution assigns each volume probability $A^{-L}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Prescribed content]
For $S\subseteq\{0,\ldots,L-1\}$ and a prescription $p:S\to\Sigma$, a volume $s$ matches $p$ on $S$ when $s(i)=p(i)$ for every $i\in S$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Indexed and contiguous occurrence]
Let $q$ be a word of length $m$ and let
\[
\iota:\{0,\ldots,m-1\}\to\{0,\ldots,L-1\}.
\]
The word $q$ occurs along $\iota$ in $s$ when $s(\iota(j))=q(j)$ for every $j$. If $m\leq L$, then $q$ occurs contiguously at start $i$ when $s(i+j)=q(j)$ for $0\leq j<m$. It occurs somewhere if such an $i\in\{0,\ldots,L-m\}$ exists.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Cyclic index]
For a cyclic word $c_0\cdots c_{n-1}$, indices are read modulo $n$. Its length-$k$ window at $i$ is $(c_i,\ldots,c_{i+k-1})$. It is a complete order-$k$ index if these windows list every word in $\Sigma^k$ exactly once.
\end{definition}

\section{Counting the universal library}

\begin{theorem}[Library cardinality]
For all nonnegative $A$ and $L$,
\[
|\mathcal L(A,L)|=A^L.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Each of the $L$ positions admits $A$ independent choices. The multiplication principle gives $A^L$. Equivalently, there are $A^L$ functions from an $L$-element set to an $A$-element set.
\end{proof}

For $A=25$ and $L=1{,}312{,}000$, the exact cardinality is $25^{1{,}312{,}000}$. Its decimal representation has
\[
1+\left\lfloor1{,}312{,}000\log_{10}25\right\rfloor=1{,}834{,}098
\]
digits.

\begin{theorem}[Constrained-content count]
Let $S$ contain $d$ distinct positions of a length-$L$ volume, and prescribe one symbol at every position in $S$. Exactly
\[
A^{L-d}
\]
volumes satisfy the prescriptions.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The symbols on $S$ are fixed, while the positions in the complement $S^c$ are free. Restriction to $S^c$ is a bijection from matching volumes to functions $S^c\to\Sigma$: restriction gives the forward map, while gluing the free assignment to the prescription gives its inverse. Since $|S^c|=L-d$, the number of assignments is $A^{L-d}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Fixed-position pattern count]
Let $q$ have length $m$, and let $\iota$ select $m$ distinct positions in a volume of length $L$. Then exactly $A^{L-m}$ volumes carry $q$ along $\iota$. For $A>0$, the uniform probability is $A^{-m}$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Injectivity makes the image of $\iota$ a set of exactly $m$ positions. Apply the constrained-content theorem and divide by $A^L$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Necessity of injectivity]
If an index is repeated, equal requested symbols produce a redundant constraint, while unequal requested symbols produce an inconsistency. Thus $L-m$ reflects $m$ distinct compatible prescriptions, not merely a list with $m$ entries.
\end{remark}

\section{Passage occurrence anywhere}

Fix a pattern $q$ of length $m\leq L$. Let $E_i$ be the set of volumes in which $q$ begins at $i$. There are $L-m+1$ starts, and each $E_i$ has size $A^{L-m}$.

\begin{theorem}[Occurrence union bound]
The number of length-$L$ volumes containing $q$ contiguously somewhere is at most
\[
(L-m+1)A^{L-m}.
\]
For $A>0$, the uniform probability satisfies
\[
\Pr(q\text{ occurs somewhere})\leq\frac{L-m+1}{A^m}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The containing set is $\bigcup_{i=0}^{L-m}E_i$. A finite union has cardinality at most the sum of the cardinalities of its members. Therefore
\[
\left|\bigcup_{i=0}^{L-m}E_i\right|
\leq\sum_{i=0}^{L-m}|E_i|
=(L-m+1)A^{L-m}.
\]
Division by the library size $A^L$ proves the probability bound.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Babel-scale bound]
For a $25$-symbol alphabet and volumes of length $1{,}312{,}000$, every fixed pattern of length $m\leq1{,}312{,}000$ satisfies
\[
\Pr(q\text{ occurs})\leq
\min\left\{1,\frac{1{,}312{,}001-m}{25^m}\right\}.
\]
\end{corollary}

The bound is not normally an equality because one volume can contain several occurrences. For binary strings of length $3$ and target $11$, the union bound is $2\cdot2^{-2}=1/2$. The exact containing set is $\{011,110,111\}$, so the probability is $3/8$. The word $111$ matches at both starts and is counted twice in the sum.

A border of length $r$ of a word is a prefix of length $r$ equal to its suffix of length $r$. Occurrences displaced by $d<m$ are compatible precisely when the overlap of length $m-d$ agrees. Exact global probabilities therefore depend on the pattern's borders or autocorrelation, not only on $A$, $L$, and $m$.

The correct prefactor in the universal bound is the number $L-m+1$ of possible placements. It is not the target length. When matches are rare and overlap effects are small, $(L-m+1)A^{-m}$ is also the natural first approximation.

\section{A complete four-symbol cyclic index}

Take $\Sigma=\{0,1,2,3\}$ and the cyclic word
\[
C=0010203112132233.
\]
Its length-two cyclic windows are
\[
00,01,10,02,20,03,31,11,12,21,13,32,22,23,33,30.
\]

\begin{theorem}[Complete mini-library index]
The sixteen cyclic length-two windows of $C$ are in bijection with $\Sigma^2$. Every two-symbol word over $\Sigma$ occurs at a unique cyclic position.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The displayed windows are pairwise distinct. Their number is sixteen, equal to $|\Sigma^2|=4^2$. An injective map between finite sets of equal cardinality is bijective, proving both existence and uniqueness.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Optimality by window capacity]
Any cyclic word whose length-two windows include all words in $\Sigma^2$ has length at least $16$. The word $C$ attains this bound.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
A cyclic word of length $n$ has exactly $n$ starting positions and at most $n$ distinct length-two windows. Covering sixteen pairs requires $n\geq16$. The preceding theorem supplies an example of length sixteen.
\end{proof}

The final pair $30$ crosses the cyclic boundary. A linear display of all sixteen windows repeats the initial symbol and becomes
\[
00102031121322330,
\]
of length seventeen. The construction indexes all two-symbol volumes, not the $4^{16}$ volumes of length sixteen.

\subsection{Overlap graphs}

For order two, form a directed graph whose vertices are alphabet symbols and whose edge $ab$ runs from $a$ to $b$. A complete cyclic index is an Eulerian circuit: every directed edge is used exactly once. Each vertex has $A$ incoming and $A$ outgoing edges.

For general order $k$, vertices are words of length $k-1$, and a length-$k$ word $x_1\cdots x_k$ is an edge from its prefix $x_1\cdots x_{k-1}$ to its suffix $x_2\cdots x_k$. An Eulerian circuit organizes all $A^k$ words with maximal overlap. Hierholzer's algorithm traverses each edge once, taking $O(A^k)$ time and storage, proportional to the output size. The explicit theorem above is the concrete four-symbol, order-two case.

\section{Algorithms for occurrence and indexing}

For small parameters, exact occurrence probability may be found by exhaustive enumeration: generate all $A^L$ volumes, compare the target at every start, and count successful volumes. Direct comparison takes $O(A^L(L-m+1)m)$ time and $O(L)$ streaming memory. This method is useful for transparent experiments but is exponential in $L$.

A scalable exact method uses a prefix automaton. Its state is the length of the longest suffix of the text read so far that is also a prefix of the pattern. Appending a symbol updates the state; reaching state $m$ records an occurrence. Dynamic programming over text positions and nonaccepting states counts avoiding strings. If $N_{\rm avoid}(L)$ denotes their number, then
\[
N_{\rm contain}(L)=A^L-N_{\rm avoid}(L).
\]
With a precomputed transition table, the running time is $O(LmA)$ and rolling memory is $O(m)$. Self-overlap is built into the transition function.

For cyclic indexes, build the overlap graph and apply Hierholzer's algorithm: follow unused outgoing edges until returning to a visited vertex, splice additional closed tours at vertices with unused edges, and read edge labels along the final Eulerian circuit. Every edge is processed once.

\section{Catalog capacity and information}

A direct fixed-width address for one member of a library of size $A^L$ requires
\[
\log_2(A^L)=L\log_2 A
\]
bits. A literal table with one independent full address for every volume requires on the order of
\[
A^L L\log_2 A
\]
bits, before delimiters and metadata. A single length-$L$ volume over the same alphabet carries only $L\log_2 A$ bits under direct fixed-width encoding, so it cannot hold that table.

This does not prohibit every possible notion of catalog. A short program may generate addresses, records may share prefixes, data may be distributed, or objects may be represented by overlapping windows. These are different decoding models. Any catalog theorem must specify what an entry is, whether entries are independently addressable, whether generation replaces storage, how delimiters work, whether cyclic overlap is allowed, and what resources the decoder may use.

The miniature cyclic index is optimal for its stated task: assigning one cyclic position to each two-symbol word. It makes no claim to store independent full addresses for all length-sixteen words.

\section{Semantic validity as language density}

The preceding theorems count symbols, not meaning. To define the probability of a valid proof, one must fix a finite encoding, decidable grammar, theorem, deterministic acceptance procedure, and any required resource bound. These choices define a language $P\subseteq\Sigma^*$.

Let $P_m=P\cap\Sigma^m$. Applying the occurrence theorem to every accepted length-$m$ string and taking another union gives
\[
\Pr(\text{some member of }P_m\text{ occurs})
\leq |P_m|(L-m+1)A^{-m}.
\]
The estimate may overcount overlapping placements and different accepted strings occurring in one volume, but it separates the syntactic penalty from the number of accepted encodings.

When an acceptance procedure has bounded memory, its language can often be represented by a finite automaton. Counts then arise from powers of a finite transition matrix, and the exponential growth rate is controlled by its spectral radius. Thus proof density becomes a precise language-density problem rather than an encoding-independent property of meaning.

\section{Applications}

For an idealized uniform DNA sequence, a fixed motif of length $m$ has fixed-position probability $4^{-m}$ and anywhere upper bound $(L-m+1)4^{-m}$. Nonuniform biological models alter the probabilities but preserve the need to account for overlap.

For random byte streams, a fixed $m$-byte signature has fixed-position probability $256^{-m}$. The union bound estimates false positives across a buffer, while automata support exact or multi-pattern scanning.

In exhaustive testing, an order-$k$ cyclic index supplies every local input configuration exactly once while minimizing carrier length. This is useful for finite-state hardware, communication channels, and sensor encoders. More broadly, the universal-library model shows why existential coverage without an index or relevance model has little retrieval value.

\section{Discussion and future work}

The exact constrained-position theorem is the core result: one factor of $A$ disappears for each distinct prescribed symbol. Contiguous occurrence anywhere is a union problem, so exactness additionally requires intersection data. Borders and autocorrelation isolate the unresolved combinatorics.

A first future direction is an exact cluster expansion from a pattern's border lattice, together with a characterization of when equal-length patterns have identical occurrence probabilities for all ambient lengths. A second is an optimal distributed-catalog theorem under an explicit block code and prefix-free delimiter model, with redundancy governed by Kraft-type constraints. A third is the general sharp cyclic-index theorem for all positive $A$ and $k$, characterizing optimal collision-free cycles as Eulerian circuits in overlap graphs. A fourth is semantic proof density under fixed grammars and resource-bounded checkers, where transfer matrices may yield computable algebraic growth rates.

\section{Conclusion}

A universal finite library contains exactly $A^L$ volumes. Prescribing $d$ distinct positions leaves $A^{L-d}$ possibilities. A fixed length-$m$ passage at one location occupies exactly fraction $A^{-m}$ of the library, while its probability of occurrence somewhere is at most $(L-m+1)A^{-m}$. Exact global probabilities depend on overlap.

The cyclic word $0010203112132233$ complements these counting results: its sixteen cyclic pairs list every two-symbol word over four symbols exactly once and attain the capacity lower bound. Together, the results show that universal inclusion solves existence but not retrieval, interpretation, or compression. Those require an index, decoder, or semantic model, each subject to the same laws of finite information.

